                        LOAD-BEARING GOTCHAS rediscovered, also relevant to
                        Phase 6/7 (any future set_channel-shaped write):

                        (i) PORT BOUNCE FOR set_channel. The Meshtastic firmware
                        buffers channel-slot changes in RAM and only commits to
                        flash when the serial session ends (DTR drop). pip-mesh
                        gets this for free because it uses `echo > $tty` (a
                        fresh open/close per write); our long-lived fd has to
                        close+reopen explicitly or the write LOOKS accepted
                        (verify-cycle handshake succeeds) but is silently
                        dropped on the next device read or reboot, and pip-mesh
                        later sees no change. set_owner and set_lora_config are
                        stored differently and do NOT need the bounce. Added
                        pn_mesh_serial_reopen() and the connection now bounces
                        the fd between the set_channel write and the verify
                        cycle. Phase 6's QR-import path will write multiple
                        channels in sequence -- bundle them in a single
                        begin_edit_settings(64)/commit_edit_settings(65)
                        transaction (per pip-mesh's --copy-channels) and one
                        bounce at the end.

                        (ii) use-after-free across the modal-destroy in the Add
                        dialog: gtk_entry_get_text returns a pointer INTO the
                        entry's buffer; destroying the dialog frees the entry.
                        The async write's internal g_strdup runs AFTER the
                        destroy, picking up freed memory -- the channel name
                        comes back as garbage on the next read-back. Fix:
                        g_strdup the entry text BEFORE destroying the dialog.
                        (psk_bytes was already safe because decode_psk_hex
                        mallocs a fresh buffer we own.)

                        (iii) UX: a 3-5s verify cycle with no visible motion
                        reads as "the app froze". Add a GtkSpinner next to the
                        action button, spin while writing=TRUE. Identity (Apply
                        owner) and Region (Apply LoRa) have the same delay and
                        would benefit from the same spinner -- not added yet
                        (user didn't ask), one-liner each when wanted.
           [x] Known Known Nodes page (out-of-band insertion between Nodes Phase
               5 and Phase 6 -- user-requested "everything works" diagnostic).
               Read-only sortable GtkTreeView over PnMeshState->nodes; the
               device's own row is bolded and pinned to the top via a hidden
               is_us primary sort key, with columns ID / Short / Long / HW /
               Role / Hops / SNR / Last heard / Battery. Last heard / SNR /
               Battery use separate numeric sort columns so the displayed
               strings ("3m ago", "—") sort by value not ASCII. Connection now
               captures the full NodeInfo set (added field defines NI_SNR=4,
               NI_LAST_HEARD=5, NI_DEVICE_METRICS=6, NI_HOPS_AWAY=9, U_ROLE=7;
               new parse_device_metrics; SeenNode extended with
               role/snr/last_heard/hops_away/battery/voltage) and publishes
               PnMeshState->nodes (GPtrArray of new public type PnMeshNode).
               format_hw_model / format_role lifted out of page-identity into
                        libpipnode-core (lib/pn-mesh-formats.{c,h}) so
                        the new page reuses the same tables. Files
                        shipped: lib/pn-mesh-formats.{c,h} (core);
                        lib/pn-mesh-page-known-nodes.{c,h} (gui).  No new
                        unit test -- the page is a pure render layer over
                        already-tested protobuf parsing; the existing
                        test-pn-mesh-pb / test-pn-mesh-frame net still holds. No
                        protocol writes; no admin frames; no ABI bump
                        (PnMeshState is internal to the dialog). Phase numbering
                        unchanged below; "Phase 5.5" would misread, so it's
                        tracked here as an inline entry.

           [x] Phase 6 Share page (headline feature). Single-QR page living
                        under the Radio tab as a third expander (Region & LoRa /
                        Channels / Share). Top: subtitle line that rephrases per
                        mode. Channel checkbox list (primary pre-checked; rows
                        are the device's non-disabled slots). Mode-toggle radio
