                        module is a fresh page on the Phase 10 pattern, and none
                        of the leftovers are must-haves for the
                        unbox-to-test-message goal. Candidates if/when this
                        comes back: Serial (Network tab); Neighbor Info,
                        Detection Sensor, Range Test, Paxcounter (Telemetry
                        tab); Canned Message, Audio, Ambient Lighting
                        (Notifications tab); Store & Forward, Remote Hardware,
                        TAK (Mesh tools tab). Sub-messages that the UI doesn't
                        expose go via the opaque-
                        bytes round-trip introduced in Phase 10
                        (PnMeshState->mqtt_map_report_settings). Notes on TODO
                        transcription slips found while descoping: "Status
                        Message" is not a real ModuleConfig sub-block; "Traffic
                        Management" is rebroadcast_mode inside DeviceConfig
                        (covered by Phase 14); "Display" is a Config sub-block
                        (was covered by Phase 13, now also postponed -- see
                        below).

           [x] Phase 12 Position + Power config tabs. Shipped as two new
                        GtkExpanders distributed across the existing 7-tab
                        layout rather than dedicated tabs -- Power sits under
                        "Device" alongside Identity, Position sits under "Radio"
                        after Region / Channels / Share. Both reuse the LoRa
                        pattern: read whole sub-block during the handshake,
                        mutate one or more fields, ship every typed field back
                        at once via AM_SET_CONFIG=34 wrapped in Config{<sub>}.

                        Wire side (pn-mesh-connection.[ch]): CFG_POSITION =2 and
                        CFG_POWER=3 dispatch in parse_config; POS_* / POW_*
                        field defines mirror the Meshtastic proto;
                        parse_position_config / parse_power_config follow
                        parse_lora_config's shape. PnMeshState gains 13 pos_*
                        and 9 pow_* fields plus have_position / have_power
                        flags. Two new writer pairs -- PnMeshPositionConfigWrite
                        + set_position_config_*, PnMeshPowerConfigWrite +
                        set_power_config_* -- share two helpers
                        (send_set_config_subblock and finish_set_config)
                        extracted from set_lora_config so the second writer
                        collapses to a per-field write loop + one call. Power's
                        adc_multiplier_override is the project's first
                        float-on-the-wire field; written via
                        pn_mesh_pb_write_fixed32_field with a union punning
                        trick, read via pn_mesh_pb_read_float.

                        UI side: pn-mesh-page-position.[c,h] and
                        pn-mesh-page-power.[c,h] are direct clones of
                        pn-mesh-page-region.[c,h]: subtitle + grid of bold-label
                        / control rows + right-aligned Apply button at the
                        bottom; ctx struct with all widget handles + status /
                        busy callbacks + writing flag; set_writing toggles every
                        control's sensitivity in one shot and emits
                        busy(TRUE/FALSE) on transition; set_state mirrors the
                        device state into the controls and stashes
                        round-trip-only fields (position_flags, the GPIOs, the
                        legacy gps_enabled vs gps_mode pair, the INA address) in
                        ctx so Apply ships them back unchanged.

                        Position page exposes GPS mode (combo: DISABLED /
                        ENABLED / NOT_PRESENT), the legacy gps_enabled boolean
                        synced from mode at Apply-time so older firmware keeps
                        consistent state, fixed_position switch, smart-broadcast
                        switch + its min-distance
                        (m) and min-interval (s) thresholds, plus
                        position_broadcast_secs and gps_update_interval. GPIO
                        assignments are deliberately NOT surfaced as controls --
                        they are board-specific and the user is more likely to
                        set them via the firmware console than the dialog;
                        round-trip preserves them anyway.

                        Power page exposes is_power_saving switch,
                        on_battery_shutdown_after_secs, adc_multiplier_override
                        (float spinner, 3-digit precision, 0 = firmware
                        default), wait_bluetooth_secs, sds_secs, ls_secs,
                        min_wake_secs; device_battery_ina_address is
                        round-tripped only. powermon_enables (field 32) is a
                        debug-only flag the dialog ignores -- same caveat the
                        LoRa page carries for spread_factor etc. It gets
                        proto3-zero-reset on Apply (rare; no end-user touched it
                        via the GUI), document if a user reports it.

                        Gotchas for the next Config sub-block writer (Phase 14):

                        (i) Float on the wire uses
                        pn_mesh_pb_write_fixed32_field with a union to
                        reinterpret bits; pn_mesh_pb_read_float reciprocates. Do
                        NOT write a varint -- proto's float wire type is fixed32
                        (wire 5), and a varint of the bit pattern would be read
                        as garbage.

                        (ii) Signed int32 fields on the wire encode negative
                        values as 10-byte sign-extended varints. The dialog's
                        smart-min-distance spinner clamps to 0 so the path is
                        never hit in practice, but the writer still casts gint32
                        -> gint64 -> guint64 before
                        pn_mesh_pb_write_varint_field so the contract is correct
                        for any signed field added later.

                        (iii) The pattern is: parse all fields the firmware
                        sends, expose only the practically-useful ones in the
                        UI, stash the rest in ctx and ship them back unchanged.
                        This avoids the proto3-defaults trap and keeps
                        board-specific / advanced settings intact without
                        bloating the page.

           [-] Phase 13 Display + Bluetooth config tabs. POSTPONED to a later
                        revision (decided 2026-05-28) -- lower payoff than
                        Phases 7/12/14 for the unbox flow; revisit alongside any
                        Phase 11 module work. DisplayConfig (13 fields:
                        screen_on_secs, flip_screen, units, use_12h_clock, ...)
                        and BluetoothConfig (3 fields: enabled, mode,
                        fixed_pin).

           [x] Phase 14 Device + Network + Security config tabs. Three new pages
                        following the LoRa/Position/Power set_config_subblock
                        pattern: parse the whole sub-block during the handshake,
                        ship every typed field back at once via AM_SET_CONFIG=34
                        wrapped in Config{<sub>}. Reuses
                        send_set_config_subblock + finish_set_config helpers
                        from Phase 12.

                        Tab layout (touched 3 of the 7 existing tabs; Mesh tools
                        + Notifications + Telemetry + Diagnostics unchanged):
                        Device: Identity / Device role & GPIO (new) / Power /
                        Security (new) Radio: Region & LoRa / Channels / Share /
                        Position Network: WiFi/Ethernet/IPv6 (new) / MQTT
                        (placeholder dropped)

                        Wire side (pn-mesh-connection.[ch]): CFG_DEVICE=1 /
                        CFG_NETWORK=4 / CFG_SECURITY=8 dispatch in parse_config;
                        DEV_*/NET_*/SEC_* field defines mirror the Meshtastic
                        Config.proto exactly. parse_device_config /
                        parse_network_config / parse_security_config follow
                        parse_lora_config's shape. PnMeshState gains 13 dev_*,
                        10 net_*, 7 sec_* fields plus have_device/have_network/
                        have_security flags. Three writer pairs --
                        PnMeshDeviceConfigWrite + set_device_config_*,
                        PnMeshNetworkConfigWrite + set_network_config_*,
                        PnMeshSecurityConfigWrite + set_security_config_* -- all
                        use the shared subblock helpers.

                        Device page: Role + RebroadcastMode combos,
                        NodeInfo-broadcast spinner, button/buzzer GPIO spinners.
                        Round-trips serial_enabled, double_tap_as_button_press,
                        deprecated is_managed, disable_triple_click, tzdef,
                        led_heartbeat_disabled, buzzer_mode unchanged.

                        Network page: WiFi switch + SSID/PSK entries (PSK
                        masked), Ethernet switch, IPv6 switch. Round-trips
                        ntp_server, address_mode, ipv4_config (opaque GBytes,
                        same pattern as mqtt_map_report_settings),
                        rsyslog_server, enabled_protocols.

                        Security page: read-only base64 view of the X25519
                        public/private keys + admin key list, switches for
                        is_managed / serial_enabled / debug_log_api_enabled /
                        admin_channel_enabled. Per TODO: "I understand the
                        risks" check gates Apply, and the check is auto-reset
                        after a successful Apply / on every set_state so the
                        next write needs an explicit re-confirmation. Key bytes
                        shipped back verbatim from PnMeshState (typing 32 random
                        bytes by hand is not useful).

                        Files shipped: lib/pn-mesh-page-device.{c,h},
                        lib/pn-mesh-page-network.{c,h},
                        lib/pn-mesh-page-security.{c,h}; deltas in
                        lib/pn-mesh-connection.{c,h}, lib/pn-mesh-dialog.c,
                        lib/Makefile.am.

                        Gotchas for any future Config sub-block writer (none yet
                        planned; Phase 11/13 are postponed):

                        (i) BYTES round-trip via GBytes. ipv4_config,
                        public_key, private_key are length-delimited
                        bytes sub-fields.  Stash them on PnMeshState as
                        GBytes* (own-on-parse, free-on-close, clear in
                        clear_state) and ref+copy into a per-call struct in the
                        async wrapper so the worker thread sees a stable input
                        even if the page rebuilds its ctx mid-flight.

                        (ii) REPEATED bytes (admin_key). Same recipe but a
                        GPtrArray-of-GBytes with g_bytes_unref as the free func,
                        allocated up front in open_sync and set_size(0) at the
                        top of each parse pass. The async wrapper deep-copies
                        the array (g_bytes_ref each entry) before handing it to
                        the thread.

                        (iii) "I understand" check. Reset to FALSE on every
                        set_state and after a successful Apply -- a sticky check
                        would let a user accidentally re-Apply a stale config
                        after a handshake gave fresh data.

       Phase 8 (Plugin extraction) is no longer tracked under TODO #29 -- moved
       out 2026-05-28: it is its own cross-cutting project (Devices menu becomes
       plugin-driven via a new PnDeviceProvider core API; the meshtastic dialog
       code migrates into the meshtastic plugin's GUI companion; PnMeshtastic
       nodes gain a "Set up device..." affordance that pre-picks the device).
       Will get its own TODO entry when picked up.

       Gotchas worth re-reading before each phase (from pip-mesh, costly to
       rediscover): always drain stale bytes before any handshake; never wait
       for an end marker, wait for silence (3s config, 5s metadata); sleep 0.5
       after every write; bare set_channel is no-reboot, commit_edit_settings
       persists+may reboot -- transactional only when needed; cache invalidation
       keyed on USB VID:PID (the tty name gets reused); frame magic is 0x94
       0xC3, length is 2-byte BIG-endian; short-name <=4 chars, long-name <=39,
       PSK exactly 32 or 64 hex chars. The pip-mesh --help string flags
       --list-messages as broken; trust the help text not the man page.

       Hard rules (do not inherit from pip-mesh): no ~/.cache/, no auto-detect
       on dialog open, no "auto-select if exactly one device", no --raw or
       --max-messages CLI modes leaking into the GUI, no global HEXDATA/PB_POS
       state -- each PnMeshConnection holds its own.

[ ] 33 Meshtastic discovery: stop treating a BARE 10c4:ea60 as a Meshtastic
       device. The VID:PID table in lib/pn-mesh-discover.c (KNOWN_DEVICES)
       matches the Heltec V3 on 10c4:ea60 -- but that is the GENERIC Silicon
       Labs CP2102 /
       CP2102N USB-UART chip, used by countless unrelated boards. In particular
       the common Sonoff/ITead Zigbee dongles (ZBDongle-P) are CP2102N ->
       10c4:ea60, so a plugged-in Zigbee coordinator shows up in the Meshtastic
       dialog as a "Heltec V3". Picking it aims the Meshtastic want_config_id
       handshake at a Z-Stack UART (root cause of the 2026-05-29 zigbee2mqtt
       CPU-spin incident: pipnode opened the shared /dev/ttyUSB and wrote
       Meshtastic frames into it; zigbee2mqtt choked on the corrupted byte
       stream).

         MITIGATED 2026-05-29 (see the in-use guard added the same day): such a
         device, while it is open by zigbee2mqtt (or anything else), now appears
         DISABLED in the list with an "in use by <proc>" note and can never be
         opened (pn_mesh_tty_in_use + TIOCEXCL in pn-mesh-serial.c). But that
         only helps WHILE the other process holds the port -- an idle, unclaimed
         CP2102 Zigbee dongle would still be mislabelled "Heltec V3" and be
         connectable.

         THE WORK (why it is hard): sysfs alone cannot disambiguate -- the
         product / manufacturer / serial strings are the chip's generic defaults
         ("CP2102 USB to UART Bridge Controller" / "Silicon Labs"), identical on
         a Heltec and a Zigbee dongle. The only reliable tell is a safe,
         read-only probe over the wire (e.g. a Meshtastic want_config_id and a
         bounded wait for a valid 0x94 0xC3 frame) -- but ANY write is exactly
         what we must NOT do to a non-Meshtastic device. Options to weigh: (a)
         mark generic-CP2102 matches as "unverified / possibly another device"
         in the UI and require an explicit user confirm before the first write;
         (b) keep a denylist of known-NON-mesh CP2102 product/serial strings;
         (c) cross-check against a system Zigbee/serial registry. None is clean;
         deferred deliberately.

[ ] 36 Stop ModemManager from probing Meshtastic serial devices. When a tty
       appears, ModemManager (root) opens it and fires AT commands -- which
       toggles DTR/RTS (rebooting the ESP32) and races pipnode's open. pipnode's
       in-use guard is a /proc scan that can't see root-owned holders, so it
       misses MM. Fix: ship a udev rule setting ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE=1 for the
       Meshtastic VID:PIDs (packaging step), and/or inhibit the device over MM's
       D-Bus API around open. Caveat: 10c4:ea60 is generic CP2102 (see #33); a
       blanket ignore also hides real CP2102 modems. Touches system config --
       weigh against the minimal-invasive-packaging line.

